{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1215250-adapting-a-wod-character-to-the-white-wholf-street-fighter-fighting-system","id":1215250,"title":"Adapting a WoD character to the White Wholf Street Fighter fighting system?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-05-04T15:26:48-07:00","id":1215250,"title":"Adapting a WoD character to the White Wholf Street Fighter fighting system?","content":"I'm running a Mage game, and one of my players is an Akashik, and I thought it would be fun (and I think they would enjoy) running a Kumite, but having there be more to the fights that the basic combat in your standard World of Darkness game. We used aspects of WoD Combat in larger combats, but the fact is that for a one-on-one tournament style fight, I think the Street Fighter system would really shine and add a novel and memorable dimension to the game. My only question is when it comes to combat stats--obviously WoD only has Brawl, Do, and Martial Arts (<i>maybe </i>depending on what game you're playing), whereas the Street Fighter breaks it down into techniques. Naturally the question then arises: if I have Brawl/Do/Martial Arts <i>x</i>, how do I convert that into stats for &quot;punch&quot; &quot;kick&quot; &quot;block&quot; etc.? I would imagine you could just get a multiplier going and allow people to spend the resultant points/chits on buying the individual technique dots... but I wouldn't even know where to begin with determining what the property multiplier would be, or any of the other practical odds and ends.<br /> <br /> Does anyone here have any ideas, or know of anyone who has already devoted the brain power and smarts to tackling this issue?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-04T22:18:12-07:00","id":1215365,"title":"","content":"Do you have WoD: Combat? It has a kind of middle ground between SF and 2nd edition WoD games. Brawl was basically Punch, Grab, and Kick, Athletics was... Athletics, Dodge was Block (going a bit retro to incorporate 1e-style dodging, where it increased soak rather than negating attack successes), and Do was everything and anything because it was a broken idea to begin with, even incorporating a few of the Focus-related maneuvers from SF as Do maneuvers. WoD characters got an awful lot more power points, though (Ability x 2).<br /> <br /> Of course, after you find a way to deal with that, you have to deal with the health level discrepancy between the two...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-14T23:57:15-07:00","id":1218819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1215250-adapting-a-wod-character-to-the-white-wholf-street-fighter-fighting-system?p=1215365#post1215365\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you have WoD: Combat?</div> </div> </div> </div> Thanks for responding! So it's funny you mention <i>Wod: Combat</i>, because my group started gaming together in like '99 or so when we were all in high school. Naturally we thought <i>WoD: Combat </i>was a wonderful idea, &quot;tables and tables of guns and melee weapons? martial arts? Kibatsumejutsu? Yes please!&quot; And, you know, we got pretty good at using it. Certainly there have been plenty of times when we did <i>not</i> use it because the flow of the game, and the difficulty and stakes of the combat were such that we just wouldn't be satisfied by throwing minis onto the hex grid and otherwise spending two or three times as long on a fight that was <i>probably</i> a foregone conclusion. So it's been a sort of progression for us, from using all of it literally all the time forever, to now, almost 20 years later, using some aspects of it (the tables, or the certain knowledge that someone knows X martial art with Y maneuvers) when seemingly appropriate and then pulling out the the top to bottom, with combat cards and everything, for big set piece battles (because these are parts of gaming we just happen to enjoy, even as our love and appreciation for the politicking as grown, something about a truly operatic scale and volume of violence is quite fun for us).<br /> <br /> I digress--the reason I want to bring ib Street Fighter is because, by the very nature of that IP and the sorts of people they were theoretically trying to pull into the hobby, it was necessary to have a formalized fighting/combat system specifically designed (by great game devs obviously) to make for interesting and tactical combat between two opponents. Even moreso that <i>WoD: Combat </i>(which, bless their hearts, really was/is just <i>okay</i>) it's a fighting mini-game of sorts. What's more, there are things about that combat system that are sooooooo appealing to long time CWoD fans--particularly the fact that you're never rolling to hit, or to dodge--it's tough to have that &quot;whiff; whiff; whiff; whiff; devastating hit/deathblow&quot; sort of thing. What's more, in <i>WoD: Combat</i> if you want &quot;move&quot; modifiers to be a thing that people have to take into consideration (i.e., thereby giving way to the tactical dimension of positional play), I have found that you basically have to design the encounter with that in mind... which is fine, but again, from reading Street Fighter, it seems like the need and desire to move is something that people will naturally just start doing aplenty once play gets going. In other words, as designed, moving around seems far more important than in <i>WoD: Combat</i>. And again, this isn't supposed to supplant the regular combat rules we use (which are absolutely fine for us, particularly after using them for so many years), but I thought it would be a special thing to do that would add memorable nuances to something that could easily just be two characters in adjacent hexes wailing on one another in largely systematically generic ways.<br /> <br /> Having said all that, I've been discussing the matter with my players, and we've begun kicking around ideas about how to adapt a WoD character to the SF system. The whole thing really does to boil down to assigning some point value multipliers to &quot;Brawl&quot; and &quot;Do&quot;, respectively, and then allowing players to distribute those into the several techniques. The issue then becomes about simulating those Do specialties/powers (not what I'm super worried about), monkeying with health (also not super worried; you start with a base of ten and then derive the adjustment up based on stamina, or other such factors), figuring out what to sub in for &quot;focus&quot; and &quot;chi&quot; (more difficult, but not apocalyptically so), and the hard part--figuring out what the multipliers should actually be for the Brawl/Do-to-Techniques conversion.<br /> <br /> Like I said, I will post whatever it is we land on. It may not end up being the best way to do this conversion, but I can assure you that will have been thoroughly deliberated upon, and hell, maybe someone sees what we did and figures the whole thing out.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sig","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2319-captotter","name":"CaptOtter","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2319_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"405"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}